WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.280
Four problems.

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:03.360
Heartburn from stomach acid.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.920
A paddock where young wheat is stunted and

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:06.640
yellow.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:08.800
A coal power station chimney.

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:11.120
And a creek running orange below an old

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:11.880
mine.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:14.400
Each one comes down to the same thing,

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:17.120
too much acid, and each is fixed by

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.080
neutralising it with a base.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.520
But you can't just pour sodium hydroxide into

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.000
a stomach, a paddock or a river.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:25.840
So how do you choose the base, and

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:27.760
what does the reaction actually do?

00:00:28.967 --> 00:00:32.566
Neutralising means using up the extra hydrogen ions,

00:00:32.647 --> 00:00:34.886
so the pH moves back towards seven.

00:00:35.607 --> 00:00:38.366
Choosing the base comes down to four questions.

00:00:38.647 --> 00:00:40.326
Is it safe where it's going?

00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:42.766
Is it cheap enough for the amount needed?

00:00:43.047 --> 00:00:45.846
Does it work gently, without overshooting and making

00:00:45.927 --> 00:00:47.446
things too basic?

00:00:47.527 --> 00:00:50.486
And what does it leave behind: a salt,

00:00:50.567 --> 00:00:52.806
water, a gas, or a solid?

00:00:54.034 --> 00:00:55.233
Start with the stomach.

00:00:55.634 --> 00:00:58.753
It makes hydrochloric acid on purpose, to digest

00:00:58.834 --> 00:01:01.473
food, but too much, or acid in the

00:01:01.554 --> 00:01:03.553
wrong place, causes heartburn.

00:01:04.114 --> 00:01:07.793
Sodium hydroxide would neutralise it, but it's caustic:

00:01:07.914 --> 00:01:09.473
it burns living tissue.

00:01:09.714 --> 00:01:12.993
Antacids use bases that barely dissolve in water,

00:01:13.074 --> 00:01:15.073
so they react mainly with the acid they

00:01:15.194 --> 00:01:15.713
meet.

00:01:15.794 --> 00:01:19.313
Magnesium hydroxide, from the acid reactions video, gives

00:01:19.394 --> 00:01:20.833
a salt and water.

00:01:20.994 --> 00:01:23.873
Calcium carbonate gives a salt, water and carbon

00:01:23.954 --> 00:01:27.473
dioxide, and that gas is why carbonate antacids

00:01:27.554 --> 00:01:28.593
can make you burp.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:30.760
Now the paddock.

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:34.360
Soil slowly turns acidic as rain washes out

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:37.640
its basic minerals, and as soil bacteria break

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:41.000
down ammonium fertilisers, like the ammonium sulfate from

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:44.840
the acid reactions video, which releases hydrogen ions.

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:48.120
Below about pH five and a half, aluminium

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:50.760
from the soil minerals starts to dissolve, and

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:53.720
dissolved aluminium damages plant roots.

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:55.480
That's why the wheat is stunted.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.900
The fix is agricultural lime, which is crushed

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:02.300
limestone, calcium carbonate.

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:05.820
Each carbonate ion accepts two hydrogen ions and

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:09.260
breaks into water and carbon dioxide, so hydrogen

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:12.460
ions are used up, and the pH rises.

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:15.260
As the soil becomes less acidic, the aluminium

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.740
goes back into solid compounds, and the roots

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:19.100
recover.

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:21.860
Limestone is cheap and safe to spread, and

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:24.300
it barely dissolves unless there's acid to react

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:27.180
with, so extra lime doesn't make the soil

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:28.140
strongly basic.

00:02:29.367 --> 00:02:31.686
The chimney is a different kind of problem.

00:02:32.247 --> 00:02:34.686
Coal contains a little sulfur, and when the

00:02:34.727 --> 00:02:38.406
coal burns, the sulfur becomes sulfur dioxide gas.

00:02:39.047 --> 00:02:41.846
In the air, sulfur dioxide dissolves in water

00:02:41.927 --> 00:02:45.126
droplets to make sulfurous acid, and some is

00:02:45.287 --> 00:02:47.526
oxidised further to sulfuric acid.

00:02:48.247 --> 00:02:50.886
That falls as acid rain, which lowers the

00:02:50.967 --> 00:02:53.606
pH of lakes and streams, and eats away

00:02:53.687 --> 00:02:55.046
limestone buildings.

00:02:56.267 --> 00:02:58.746
So power stations remove it before it leaves

00:02:58.827 --> 00:03:00.426
the chimney, in a scrubber.

00:03:00.827 --> 00:03:02.986
The exhaust gas passes up through a spray

00:03:03.067 --> 00:03:06.666
of calcium hydroxide slurry, a solid base stirred

00:03:06.787 --> 00:03:07.546
through water.

00:03:07.787 --> 00:03:12.106
Calcium hydroxide plus sulfur dioxide gives calcium sulfite

00:03:12.187 --> 00:03:12.826
and water.

00:03:13.307 --> 00:03:16.106
Sulfur dioxide has no hydrogen of its own,

00:03:16.227 --> 00:03:17.946
so it can't donate a proton.

00:03:18.427 --> 00:03:21.386
It's an acidic oxide: a non-metal oxide that

00:03:21.467 --> 00:03:23.786
makes an acid in water and reacts with

00:03:23.867 --> 00:03:25.306
bases to make a salt.

00:03:25.547 --> 00:03:28.186
In the slurry's water it first makes sulfurous

00:03:28.347 --> 00:03:31.306
acid, and the hydroxide neutralises that.

00:03:31.347 --> 00:03:33.786
The calcium sulfite is a solid, so it's

00:03:33.867 --> 00:03:36.346
collected, and it's often turned into gypsum for

00:03:36.387 --> 00:03:37.146
plasterboard.

00:03:38.367 --> 00:03:39.646
Now the orange creek.

00:03:40.207 --> 00:03:43.966
When mining exposes rock containing iron sulfide, air

00:03:44.047 --> 00:03:47.006
and water slowly turn it into sulfuric acid

00:03:47.247 --> 00:03:49.486
and dissolved iron ions.

00:03:49.607 --> 00:03:52.206
This acid mine drainage can reach pH three

00:03:52.287 --> 00:03:53.166
or lower.

00:03:53.487 --> 00:03:55.806
In a river, the acid harms fish and

00:03:55.887 --> 00:03:59.566
insects directly, and it keeps toxic metals dissolved,

00:03:59.647 --> 00:04:01.326
where living things can take them up.

00:04:02.534 --> 00:04:06.533
Treatment plants add hydrated lime, calcium hydroxide, to

00:04:06.574 --> 00:04:07.773
the drainage water.

00:04:08.054 --> 00:04:12.053
Its hydroxide ions neutralise the hydrogen ions, and

00:04:12.134 --> 00:04:15.013
as the pH rises, the metal ions meet

00:04:15.094 --> 00:04:19.013
more hydroxide and form insoluble hydroxides, like the

00:04:19.134 --> 00:04:22.613
rust brown iron hydroxide precipitate you've seen before.

00:04:22.694 --> 00:04:25.813
The solid settles as sludge, so one treatment

00:04:25.894 --> 00:04:29.013
fixes the pH and removes the metals.

00:04:29.094 --> 00:04:31.413
Why not limestone, like the paddock?

00:04:31.494 --> 00:04:34.293
It works slowly and stops near neutral, but

00:04:34.374 --> 00:04:36.453
some metals only drop out at a higher

00:04:36.534 --> 00:04:37.493
pH.

00:04:37.574 --> 00:04:40.933
Hydrated lime dissolves more and acts faster, so

00:04:41.014 --> 00:04:43.173
the plant controls the dose.

00:04:43.254 --> 00:04:45.813
Waste that's too basic, from places like cement

00:04:45.974 --> 00:04:48.933
works, goes the other way: it's neutralised with

00:04:49.014 --> 00:04:51.173
an acid, or carbon dioxide.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:55.040
So here's the idea that ties these together.

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:58.720
Every fix leaves something behind, and that often

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:00.120
decides the choice.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:03.040
Antacids leave a salt and water, plus carbon

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:04.920
dioxide from carbonates.

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:08.160
Lime in soil leaves calcium ions, water and

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:09.440
carbon dioxide.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:12.160
The scrubber leaves a solid you can collect,

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:14.720
and mine treatment leaves a metal sludge.

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:18.080
And for any application, give four things: the

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:21.520
problem, the treatment, the equation, and the effect

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:22.560
on the pH.

00:05:23.767 --> 00:05:24.406
Your turn.

00:05:25.047 --> 00:05:27.206
A lake has become acidic as a result

00:05:27.287 --> 00:05:29.926
of acid rain, and its fish population is

00:05:30.007 --> 00:05:30.966
declining.

00:05:31.287 --> 00:05:33.606
Powdered limestone is added to the lake.

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:36.966
Explain how the limestone raises the pH of

00:05:37.007 --> 00:05:37.766
the lake water.

00:05:38.327 --> 00:05:40.886
Include a balanced equation in your answer.

00:05:41.207 --> 00:05:42.566
Pause here to answer.

00:05:52.967 --> 00:05:55.846
Each carbonate ion in the limestone accepts two

00:05:55.927 --> 00:05:58.606
hydrogen ions from the lake water and breaks

00:05:58.647 --> 00:06:01.766
into water and carbon dioxide: C a C

00:06:01.847 --> 00:06:04.726
O three plus two H plus gives C

00:06:04.847 --> 00:06:08.086
a two plus, water and C O two.

00:06:08.167 --> 00:06:10.966
Hydrogen ions are used up, so their concentration

00:06:11.127 --> 00:06:13.766
falls and the pH rises.

00:06:13.847 --> 00:06:16.726
Saying that calcium ions neutralise the acid is

00:06:16.767 --> 00:06:17.846
the trap.

00:06:17.927 --> 00:06:21.126
The calcium ions don't react with hydrogen ions.

00:06:21.207 --> 00:06:22.806
The carbonate does the work.

00:06:24.034 --> 00:06:24.673
Last one.

00:06:25.314 --> 00:06:28.433
Some power stations remove sulfur dioxide from flue

00:06:28.514 --> 00:06:32.833
gas using powdered limestone, calcium carbonate, instead of

00:06:32.914 --> 00:06:34.113
calcium hydroxide.

00:06:34.674 --> 00:06:37.873
The products are calcium sulfite and one gas.

00:06:38.514 --> 00:06:41.073
Write a balanced equation for this reaction, and

00:06:41.154 --> 00:06:44.273
identify one disadvantage of using limestone.

00:06:44.754 --> 00:06:46.113
Pause here to answer.

00:06:56.534 --> 00:07:00.933
Calcium carbonate plus sulfur dioxide gives calcium sulfite

00:07:01.014 --> 00:07:02.853
plus carbon dioxide.

00:07:02.894 --> 00:07:05.573
It's already balanced, and there's no water, because

00:07:05.654 --> 00:07:08.213
neither reactant has any hydrogen.

00:07:08.254 --> 00:07:12.213
The disadvantage: the scrubber releases carbon dioxide, a

00:07:12.374 --> 00:07:15.493
greenhouse gas, even though limestone is cheaper than

00:07:15.574 --> 00:07:17.253
calcium hydroxide.

00:07:17.454 --> 00:07:19.813
Writing water as a product is the classic

00:07:19.894 --> 00:07:20.693
slip.

00:07:20.814 --> 00:07:22.533
Check the hydrogens first.

00:07:23.734 --> 00:07:27.413
So for any neutralisation application: name what's too

00:07:27.494 --> 00:07:30.533
acidic or too basic, choose a treatment that's

00:07:30.734 --> 00:07:34.053
safe, cheap and gentle, write the equation, and

00:07:34.134 --> 00:07:36.173
say what happens to the pH and what's

00:07:36.214 --> 00:07:37.493
left behind.

00:07:38.054 --> 00:07:40.733
Next: why some acids are strong and others

00:07:40.774 --> 00:07:43.733
are weak, and how that differs from concentration.

00:07:44.934 --> 00:07:46.373
Here are the key notes.

00:07:46.614 --> 00:07:48.533
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:48.614 --> 00:07:49.413
to copy them.
